# Line endings belong to the repository, not to whoever cloned it.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true, so on a Windows machine the index
# comes out LF by accident of an installer default rather than by decision. A
# contributor without that default commits CRLF instead, and from then on every
# clone on Linux or macOS reads files rewritten end to end with nothing in them
# changed. Worse, an editor that appends LF to a checked-out CRLF file leaves it
# mixed, which no diff explains.
#
# This takes the machine out of the decision.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Not a preference: cmd.exe reads a batch file line by line while it runs, and
# is known to misbehave on LF alone around labels and goto.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
